;; Model-specific register, read half.
;;
;; This is MECHANISM leaving C, like `pci-config-read` and unlike everything
;; before it -- but it does not leave empty-handed. Three kernel files each
;; carried their own private copy of the same four lines:
;;
;;   static uint64_t read_msr(uint32_t msr) {
;;     uint32_t lo, hi; __asm__ volatile("rdmsr" : "=a"(lo), "=d"(hi) : "c"(msr));
;;     return ((uint64_t)hi << 32) | lo;
;;   }
;;
;; apic.c, paging.c and process.c. Three copies of an instruction that can read
;; any of 2^32 registers, each reachable by whatever its caller computed. What
;; moves here is the instruction; what is ADDED is that the set of registers it
;; can reach is now a reviewed list, enforced by compiler-emitted code, rather
;; than the argument.
;;
;; THE ADMITTED SET -- derived from the callers, not from the architecture
;;
;; Every index below is one this kernel has an actual, present reason to read.
;; The list was built by reading the three files, not by transcribing the SDM:
;;
;;   27          0x1b        IA32_APIC_BASE   apic.c:35,37 -- read to check the
;;                                            xAPIC window is where the page
;;                                            tables put it, then written back
;;                                            with the global enable bit.
;;   3221225600  0xc0000080  IA32_EFER        paging.c:140 sets NXE (bit 11) so
;;                                            the PTE_NX this file writes into
;;                                            every entry is not reserved;
;;                                            process.c:127 sets SCE (bit 0) so
;;                                            `syscall` decodes at all, and
;;                                            :132 reads it back as evidence.
;;   3221225601  0xc0000081  IA32_STAR        process.c:128,133 -- the CS/SS
;;                                            selector pair for syscall/sysret.
;;   3221225602  0xc0000082  IA32_LSTAR       process.c:129,134 -- the 64-bit
;;                                            syscall entry point.
;;   3221225604  0xc0000084  IA32_FMASK       process.c:130,135 -- the RFLAGS
;;                                            bits cleared on entry.
;;
;; Five. Note what is NOT here, because the gaps are the point:
;;
;;   * 0xc0000083 IA32_CSTAR is absent, and it sits BETWEEN two admitted
;;     indices. It is the compatibility-mode syscall target; this kernel enters
;;     user mode in 64-bit mode only and never programs it. An admission list
;;     derived from "the SYSCALL MSRs" as a range would have included it. This
;;     one was derived from grep, so it does not.
;;   * 0xc0000100/0101/0102 (FS_BASE, GS_BASE, KERNEL_GS_BASE) are absent.
;;     There is no `swapgs` anywhere in this tree and no per-CPU base is ever
;;     programmed -- smp.c reads the AP's identity from the APIC ID register
;;     over MMIO instead. If per-CPU state ever arrives, these get added HERE,
;;     with the same review, rather than appearing at a call site.
;;   * 0x10 (TSC), 0x1a0 (MISC_ENABLE), 0x277 (PAT), 0x3a (FEATURE_CONTROL)
;;     and the rest of the architectural set are absent for the same reason:
;;     nothing in this kernel reads them today.
;;
;; TWO CALLERS THAT DO NOT MOVE
;;
;; multiboot/entry.S:82 and kernel/ap_trampoline.S:31 also execute `rdmsr`, on
;; EFER, and they STAY as assembly. Both run in 32-bit protected mode, before
;; the long-mode transition they are themselves performing -- there is no
;; 64-bit SysV frame there to call a Kotoba object from, and the object is
;; 64-bit code. The AP trampoline's copy is genuinely per-CPU; this object's
;; callers are not (see FUEL).
;;
;; WHAT A REFUSED READ RETURNS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A SENTINEL
;;
;; A refused index performs NO `rdmsr`. That is the whole guarantee, and it is
;; the part that is worth having: an index this kernel has no reason to read is
;; not read, so it cannot fault (a reserved MSR raises #GP, and at paging-init
;; time the kernel's own IDT is installed and would take it), and no register
;; this kernel has not reviewed reaches a caller.
;;
;; The RETURN VALUE cannot carry that news. 0 is a perfectly legal MSR value --
;; IA32_FMASK is 0 at reset, IA32_EFER is 0 on a machine that has not yet
;; enabled long mode, and IA32_STAR is 0 until something writes it. So is -1.
;; So is every other 64-bit pattern. There is no value to reserve, and
;; inventing one would be worse than admitting this, because a caller that
;; tested for it would be testing for a value the hardware can also produce.
;;
;; So: this object returns 0 on refusal and DOES NOT SIGNAL. A caller cannot
;; distinguish "refused" from "the register holds zero" and must not try. What
;; a caller must do instead is one of:
;;
;;   * pass only admitted indices -- which every caller in this kernel does,
;;     because the list above was derived from them, so the refusal path is
;;     unreachable from the current tree; or
;;   * for a write, use `aiueos-msr-write`, whose return IS a status rather
;;     than a value (1 admitted, 0 refused) and therefore CAN say no.
;;
;; 0 is chosen over -1 because of what the two call sites that actually
;; EXAMINE a read do with it. Both fail closed on zero and only on zero:
;;
;;   apic.c:  (base & 0xfffff000) != 0xfee00000  -- 0 fails, boot stage refuses
;;   process.c: read back == the value just written -- 0 fails all four
;;
;; whereas -1 passes neither but also masks to 0xfffff000 in the first, which
;; is a wrong address rather than an obviously absent one. paging.c is the one
;; site that does not examine its read -- it ORs NXE in and writes back -- and
;; a refusal there would clear LME and SCE. That is exactly why EFER is on the
;; admitted list and why the list is reviewed here rather than trusted there.
;;
;; SIGNEDNESS -- i64 is signed, the C is unsigned, and here that is free
;;
;; The recurring bug in this tree is arithmetic on a value whose bit 63 the
;; hardware controls. This object is arranged so that there is none:
;;
;;   * THE INDEX is only ever compared with `=`, against positive literals,
;;     and is never masked, divided or shifted. `=` on i64 is exact bit
;;     equality, so it is sign-agnostic by construction. The C passes a
;;     `uint32_t`, which zero-extends into a non-negative i64, so no admitted
;;     index can arrive negative; and an index that IS negative -- reachable
;;     over this object's own i64 signature even though no current caller can
;;     produce one -- equals none of the five literals and is refused. The
;;     largest literal, 3221225604 = 0xc0000084, is well under 2^63 and is
;;     positive as an i64.
;;
;;   * THE VALUE IS NEVER TOUCHED. `(kernel-read-msr index)` is returned
;;     directly: no comparison, no mask, no `quot`. This is deliberate and is
;;     the reason nothing here needs the mask-before-divide care that
;;     `vtd-admit` needs. The backend assembles the result as
;;     `(rdx << 32) | rax` over two 32-bit destination writes, each of which
;;     zeroes the upper half of its 64-bit register, so the i64 that comes out
;;     is the hardware's 64 bits EXACTLY. An MSR with bit 63 set -- and
;;     IA32_APIC_BASE has bit 63 (the x2APIC-era reserved/extended range) on
;;     machines with more than 4 GiB of physical address space, while EFER and
;;     the SYSCALL MSRs are 64-bit registers whose high halves are live --
;;     arrives as a NEGATIVE i64. That negative number is the correct answer:
;;     the bit pattern is right, and `:i64` is signed. Returning it unexamined
;;     is what keeps it right; the C receives it back into a `uint64_t` and
;;     the pattern is unchanged.
;;
;; FUEL -- 1 per call, 7 calls per boot, no replenish tier needed
;;
;; This object is deliberately absent from `bounded-memory?` in kotoba-native's
;; elf64.clj, so its wrapper emits no `mov qword [r9+8], N` replenish and it
;; spends from the 512 its own `.data` context is initialised with. Measured
;; from the emitted object rather than estimated: the disassembly contains
;; exactly two `decq 0x8(%r9)` sites, one at the entry of this function and one
;; at the entry of `main`, which the kernel never calls. There is no helper
;; `defn`, no recursion and no walk, so every invocation costs exactly ONE unit
;; on every path -- admitted and refused alike, since the `decq` precedes the
;; comparison.
;;
;; The call count is seven, and it is seven on every boot because all three
;; callers are one-shot BSP initialisers reached from kernel/main.c:
;;
;;   paging.c:140    aiueos_paging_initialize      main.c:331     1 read
;;   apic.c:35       aiueos_apic_timer_initialize  main.c:430     1 read
;;   process.c:127   syscall_transport_initialize  main.c:709     1 read
;;   process.c:132-135  ... the same function's evidence check    4 reads
;;
;; 7 of 512, a factor of 73 in hand. Adding a helper `defn` would cost one more
;; unit per call and still fit; a walk would need a tier, and this has neither.
;;
;; The SMP path was checked specifically and does NOT multiply this.
;; `aiueos_ap_entry` in smp.c reads the AP's APIC ID from MMIO
;; (`aiueos_apic_mmio_base[0x20/4]`) and then halts -- it never calls
;; `aiueos_apic_timer_initialize`, which main.c invokes once, before
;; `aiueos_smp_start_application_processor`, on the BSP. The only per-CPU MSR
;; access in this tree is the trampoline's own assembly, which does not come
;; through here.

(defn aiueos-msr-read [index]
  (if (or (= index 27)
          (= index 3221225600)
          (= index 3221225601)
          (= index 3221225602)
          (= index 3221225604))
    (kernel-read-msr index)
    ;; Refused: no `rdmsr` is executed. 0 is not a sentinel -- see above.
    0))

(defn main [] 0)
